-- title:
--   The long-edge form of the path properties implies the plain form
-- statement:
--   Fix $d \in \mathbb{N}$ and real numbers $\vartheta$ (an apex angle) and $R_0$ (a distance threshold). Call a configuration $\Gamma$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$ *$\vartheta$-bounded* when $0 < \vartheta$ and every double cone it assigns has apex angle at least $\vartheta$, i.e. $\vartheta \le (\Gamma x).\mathrm{apex}$ for all $x$. Write $V^{\Gamma}[x] = x + |\Gamma(x)|$ for the cone of $\Gamma$ based at $x$, and let the graph attached to $\Gamma$ have vertex set $\mathbb{Z}^d$, distinct lattice points $x,y$ being adjacent when $y \in V^{\Gamma}[x]$ or $x \in V^{\Gamma}[y]$.
--
--   Given $N, M \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, $\mathrm{PathProps}(\Gamma, N, M, \lambda)$ asserts the existence of a family of walks $p_{xy}$ in that graph, one from $x$ to $y$ for every ordered pair of lattice points, with $\operatorname{length}(p_{xy}) \le N$, with $\#\{(x,y) : e \in \operatorname{edges}(p_{xy})\} \le M$ for every unoriented edge $e$, and with
--   $$\lambda^{-1}\lVert x-y\rVert \;\le\; \lVert a-b\rVert \;\le\; \lambda\,\lVert x-y\rVert$$
--   for every dart $(a,b)$ of every $p_{xy}$. The predicate $\mathrm{PathPropsLong}(\Gamma,N,M,\lambda,R_0)$ is the same with the extra requirement $R_0 < \lVert a-b\rVert$ on every dart. Then let
--
--   * $\mathrm{PathPropsHolds}(d, \vartheta, R_0)$ be the assertion of Theorem 5.15:
--   $$\exists\, N, M \in \mathbb{N},\ \lambda \in \mathbb{R}: \quad 0 < N,\ 0 < M,\ R_0 \le \lambda, \ \text{ and } \ \mathrm{PathProps}(\Gamma, N, M, \lambda) \text{ for every } \vartheta\text{-bounded } \Gamma;$$
--   * $\mathrm{PathPropsLongHolds}(d, \vartheta, R_0)$ be the same statement with $\mathrm{PathProps}(\Gamma,N,M,\lambda)$ replaced by $\mathrm{PathPropsLong}(\Gamma, N, M, \lambda, R_0)$.
--
--   Then
--   $$\mathrm{PathPropsLongHolds}(d, \vartheta, R_0) \implies \mathrm{PathPropsHolds}(d, \vartheta, R_0).$$
--
--   This is simply the forgetting of the strengthening: the "long" version is what the geometric construction actually delivers, while the plain version is the interface that the statement of Theorem 5.15 exports. The strengthening matters because the chaining argument applies a kernel comparison that is only valid for pairs at distance more than $R_0$, whereas the plain claim bounds an edge below only by $\lambda^{-1}\lVert x-y\rVert$, which for $\lVert x-y\rVert$ just above $R_0$ is far weaker; in the plain version $R_0$ survives only through the side condition $R_0 \le \lambda$, which is inherited unchanged.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis whatsoever is placed on $\vartheta$ and $R_0$ at this level — they are arbitrary reals, and any constraints they must satisfy are internal to the two predicates (the positivity of $\vartheta$, for instance, being part of $\vartheta$-boundedness).
